It does not really extend to all Nordic traditions 
or tongues.  Austria, OTOH, is maybe the country 
most fond of titles.  Also in eastern Europe, there 
are some strong traditions for using academic titles; 
and please note that Boudewijn is Dutch, which is 
neither German nor Nordic (AFAIK, CMIIW ;-)).  

> Here in America, "Mr. Dr.
> Professor", the equivalent of a common Germanic
> university teacher's title, 

This is not correct.  The 'Dr.' is the notion for 
the academic degree of a PhD.  (In fact, it becomes 
part of your name in Germany, although this is only 
regarded in official writings and formal introductions.)  
'Professor' is the title for a permanent university teacher.  
[This is roughly comparable to a 'full professorship', AFAIK.]  
Further, it would be "Herr Professor Schmutzenberger", if you 
wanted to be that formal.  If you wanted to be extremely 
formal, you'd say "Herr Professor Dr. Schmutzenberger", 
although few people are actually using this.  A professor 
is almost always a Dr. anyway (exceptions are e.g. engineering 
departments (in particular in earlier times), and of course, 
fine arts).  Most of the time, however, most university teachers 
do not insist on the title 'professor' when addressed, AFAIK.  
I know only about one professor who was irritated when 
adressed without his 'proper' title.  

The question of being adressed as 'Schmutzenberger' or 
'Herr Schmutzenberger', OTOH, is something completely 
different.  Although it is not unusual to refer to 
'Schmutzenberger' simply when talking about him, it is 
almost insulting to call someone 'Schmutzenberger' when 
talking to him.  

